# Expressly [[spoken]] rather than [[written]]; [[oral]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''verbal''' contract}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''verbal''' testimony}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Charles Dickens| title=w:Great Expectations| year=1861| passage=You can't have '''verbal''' communication with a man in New South Wales, you know.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=George Orwell|journal=Tribune| title=What is Fascism?| year=1944| passage=I am not speaking of the '''verbal''' use of the term 'Fascist'. I am speaking of what I have seen in print.}}
# {{lb|en|grammar}} Derived from, or having the nature of a [[verb]].
#: {{syn|en|rhematic}}
# {{lb|en|grammar}} Used to form a verb.
# Capable of [[speech]].
#: {{ant|en|preverbal}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=Avril V. Brereton; Bruce J. Tonge|title=Pre-schoolers with autism|page=55
|passage=How do these language problems affect the behaviour of '''verbal''' children?}}
# Word for word.
#: {{syn|en|literal|verbatim}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''verbal''' translation}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Abounding with words; [[verbose]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|2|3|passage=You put me to forget a lady’s manners<br>By being so '''verbal'''; and learn now, for all,<br>That I, which know my heart, do here pronounce <br>By th’ very truth of it, I care not for you}}

